ÔªøVoxel size (mm)
Smaller values increase precision on meshing, but 
require more GPU memory and longer processing times.
Typical values are between 0.01mm and 1.0mm. 
Increase processing voxel size if IceSL reports an 
out-of-GPU-memory error.ÔªøExport to mesh
Produces a mesh [.stl]
The mesh can be exported using different meshing methods.
Currently there are two main approaches; (1) directly
meshing the visual output from IceSL or (2) assembling
the slice contours and producing a mesh from this.
The latter is useful to export the mesh to an external 3D
printing software that requires a closed mesh.ÔªøProcessing tile size (mm)
Size of a tile during processing. Decrease to 
reduce GPU memory consumption, in particular if 
slicing generates an out of GPU memory error. 
Typical values are between 5 mm and 100 mm.ÔªøDiscretization step in xy (mm)
Smaller values increase precision on paths, but 
require more GPU memory and longer processing times.
Typical values are between 0.01mm and 0.1mm. 
Decrease processing tile size if the slicer reports 
an out of GPU memory error.ÔªøMeshing method to use
The following methods are available:

-Marching cubes: Constructs a mesh from the scene
using the marching cubes method (SIGGRAPH, 1987).

-Dual Contouring: Constructs a mesh from the scene
using the dual contouring method (SIGGRAPH, 2002).

-Mesh ribbons: Produces a mesh by only producing
vertical sides from the slice contours. While
completely open, the mesh can be processed success-
fully by most external slicers. 

-Mesh slices: Produces a watertight mesh from the
slices. The mesh is assembled from the slice contours.
This process is somewhat slow and not guaranteed 
to succeed, so prefer ribbons whenever possible.ÔªøSlice thickness (mm)
Slice thickness in the Z direction.ÔªøSettings
Settings
ÔªøNumber of contours in tower brim
Number of contours on the brim of the prime/wipe tower
(first layer). As the tower is usually thin and has to resist
wipe motions from the extruder, it is best to use multiple 
contours on its brim (e.g. 10).ÔªøMax. travel length w/out retracting
Maximum travel length in millimiteres (mm) without
performing a retraction. In many cases IceSL can avoid
retraction, for instance when traveling within
a solid layer. This setting controls the maximum
allowed length without any retraction. It will force
retraction to occur on any travel longer than the
selected value. This takes priority over any other
criterion. Setting a value of 0.0 will force to
always retract, while a large value will minimize
retraction. It is recommended to use large values.ÔªøFill tiny gaps
Fill the small gaps left by covers and shells.ÔªøNumber of brim contours (integer)
Number of contours forming the brim surrounding 
the object to be printed.Contour deviation in xy plane.
Maximum deviation (mm) of simplified contours in
the xy plane.ÔªøBed size x (mm)
Size (in millimeters) of printing bed, left to right.ÔªøGround anchor diameters (mm)
Controls the size of pillar 'feet'; where supports
connect to the print bed. Increase if the pillars
detach from the print bed.ÔªøProcessing tile size (mm)
Size of a tile during processing. Decrease to 
reduce GPU memory consumption, in particular if 
slicing generates an out of GPU memory error. 
Typical values are between 5 mm and 100 mm.ÔªøNumber of nested cavitiesÔªøEnable active temperature control
When printing with multiple materials, only one extruder
is active at a given time. Active temperature control
is specifically designed to avoid material to degrade
and ooze from idle extruders. The idle extruder is
dynamically cooled down and heated during printing,
such that it is ready when needed and cooled when
not needed. Adjust extruder parameters
idle_extruder_temp_degree_c and extruder_degrees_per_sec
when using this feature.ÔªøSupports
Generates a support structure in overhanging areas. 
This is often necessary on complex geometries.ÔªøDifferent thickness first layerEnable shield
Generates a shield structure surrounding the part. 
It serves two purposes. On single material print it 
allows to keep hot air enclosed around the part and
reduce warping. On multi-material print, it serves as
a wipe shield to catch oozing filament before it reaches
the part. 
Whether to use a shield or a tower for multi-material 
prints depends on your printer: if the printer has nozzles 
that lift up/down, or a single nozzle with multiple 
filaments, use a tower. If it is a standard setup with
multiple fixed nozzles side by side, use a shield.ÔªøBrush onto which cavity is applied
Select here the brush that was used to emit the
shape to be hollowed. Leave unchanged if unsure.ÔªøPillar size (mm)
Controls the size pillar sides. Pillars are made of
two walls in a cross shape. Increasing this parameter
enlarges the cross. Increase if the pillars do not
print well or seem too fragile.ÔªøHeated bed temperature (C)
Temperature (in Celsius) of the heated bed when 
printing. Adjust depending on your material to
increase adhesion and limit warping.ÔªøDistance shield - print
ÔªøMin. space between supported points (mm)
Minimal spacing (in millimeters) between the contact
points covering overhanging surfaces.
Lowering this value makes the support denser,  
increasing the quality of bottom surfaces but 
making the support more difficult to remove.ÔªøSpacing in-between raft lines (mm)
Space in millimeters (mm) between the lines of the 
raft top layers. The raft layers below use twice 
that spacing.ÔªøInfill type
Infill type for a given brush. IceSL is packed
with four options listed bellow. It is possible
to add a custom infill by writing its specifi-
cation (see 'README.md' file in 'icesl-infillers'
folder):

-Default: Tetrahedral infills (best for the
majority of situations).

-Progressive: Changes to infill percentage
produce smooth variations on the output.

-Cubic: Cubic infills with no smoothing
of infill percentage variations.

-Polyfoam: Sponge-like structure infill. See other
settings below for more control of the output.ÔªøEnable cooling fan
Enable the filament cooling fan for all
layers.ÔªøBrim
The brim adds contours around the object, on the 
base layer. This is useful to prep the nozzle and 
enlarge the object's base for increased adhesion 
(use a distance between brim and print of 0.0 mm).ÔªøThicken thin features
Enable to thicken and preserve thin features. 
By default, thin features, smaller that the nozzle 
diameter, will be removed. This option uses an 
experimental algorithm to preserve such features, 
thickening them so that they can be printed.ÔªøFan speed percentage
Filament cooling fan speed, as a percentage
of its full speed.ÔªøPaint mixing field between extruders
This brings up the color mixing interface.
IMPORTANT: Color mixing is NOT supported if layer thickness
is set per-layer.ÔªøEnable curved covers
Enable the generation of covers that are curved
with respect to the shape.ÔªøBed size y (mm)
Size (in millimeters) of printing bed, front to back.ÔªøPaint where to interdict supportÔªøFirst layer height (mm)ÔªøCover thickness (mm)ÔªøDistance between brim and print (mm)
Distance in millimeters (mm) between the surrounding
brim and the object to be printed. Use 0.0 to attach
the brim to the base layer and stabilize the print.ÔªøFan speed percentage for bridges
Filament cooling fan speed when printing bridges,
as a percentage of its full speed.ÔªøFlow multiplier
Material flow multiplier when printing with this 
brush. Reduce or increase to artificially push more
or less plastic. Can be used to produce fragile
parts (e.g. soluble supports).ÔªøInfill percentage (%)
Infill percentage for this brush. 100 is fully
dense while 0 produces no infill. The infill 
pattern forms a strong 3D structure.ÔªøTravel speed (mm/sec)
Travel speed (millimeters per second) when not
printing. High travel speeds reduce print time.ÔªøPrint speed on perimeters (mm/sec)
Printing speed (millimeters per second) for the 
perimeters. As the perimeters are the most visible
part of a print, this typically uses a lower speed
for a smoother surface finish.ÔªøThickening ratio for thin featuresÔªøSpeed multiplier
Printing speed multiplier for this brush. This can
be used to change the printing speed locally,
using different brushes in different parts of the
object.ÔªøNumber of shells (integer)
Number of internal contours parallel to the perimeter. 
Increase to thicken the print hull. Setting the number 
of shells to a large value produces only parallel 
contours, while a value of 0 removes all contours 
but the perimeter.ÔªøExtruder
Extruder to use for this brush. Brushes can be 
used to specify which extruder to use in different
parts of a print. From a lua script, use emit(s,b)
with s the shape and b the id of the brush.ÔªøHeating/cooling rate (C/sec)
This parameter is needed when using active temperature
control. It tells IceSL how fast an extruder is able to
cool down and heat. Most often, cooling is faster than
heating so use the slower of both. Heating is often
impacted by room temperature: you may want to
lower this value in cool environments.
If you notice a pause after an extruder swap, lower
this value.ÔªøTravel only in straight line
By default IceSL attempts to stay within printed 
surfaces while traveling. This prevents stringing
to appear and reduces the need for retraction.
One downside is that travel moves take longer, as 
they follow the shape outlines. Enabling this 
option will force to travel only in straight line.
This can be useful on very complex shapes with 
many small holes, but is otherwise not recommended.ÔªøZ-lift in travel on extruder swap (mm)
After swapping between extruders, when traveling back 
from the swap location or prime/wipe tower, an up/down
z motion can be used to prevent the extruder from
wiping its nozzle against the part perimeter. A small
value is usually sufficient, e.g. 1 millimeter.ÔªøTarget temperature when idle (C)
This parameter is needed when using active temperature
control. This is the target temperature of the idle extruder.
Select the highest value that does not produce oozing
and ensures the material will not degrade in the nozzle.ÔªøDiscretization step in xy (mm)
Smaller values increase precision on paths, but 
require more GPU memory and longer processing times.
Typical values are between 0.01mm and 0.1mm. 
Decrease processing tile size if the slicer reports 
on out of GPU memory error.ÔªøMixing ratios
ÔªøFilament diameter (mm)
Diameter (in millimeters) of the filament used 
by this extruder.ÔªøPrint perimeter
Enable printing the perimeters for this brush. 
Disabling the perimeters and using 0 shells produce
a print with only the infill. This is also useful
for brushes which are completely inside another.Overhang angle
Minimum angle (degrees) between an infill wall and
the xy plane.ÔªøNumber of contours on shieldÔªøEnable single path fill
Allows the slicer to fill thin gaps using a single
deposition path (varying width) instead of a zigzag.
ÔªøFilament retract (mm)
Length of filament retracted to prevent oozing
during travel motions.ÔªøRetraction length after swap (mm)
Specfies filament retraction length after swapping 
beteewn extruders.
Greater lengths prevent oozing from the idle extruders.ÔªøPrint speed (mm/sec)
Default printing speed (millimeters per second) for 
most paths (shells, infill, raft, brim).ÔªøNumber of covers (integer)
Number of fully filled layers bellow/above print 
tops/bottoms. A typical value is 6.ÔªøCavity
Hollows the shape to be printed. Enable this 
option to considerably reduce the material used and
shorten the printing time. To avoid the print being 
structurally weak, we recommend using at least one
shell.ÔªøNozzle diameter (mm)
Diameter of the exit hole of the nozzle installed 
on the printer.ÔªøVolume for purging the extruder (mm3)
Material volume to purge from the extruder
before printing.ÔªøExtruder for infillÔªøSlice to GCode
Produces instructions for filament 3D printers [.gcode]ÔªøFlow multiplier for supportsÔªøAvoid traveling within top (visible) covers
When enabled, the travel optimizer will avoid 
traveling within the top (visible) covers. 
Enable if the extruder leaves a visible mark on 
flat top surfaces. Leave disabled for faster 
processing if the print has few top flat surfaces,
or if aesthetics are not a concern.ÔªøSlicing algorithm
Algorithm used to slice the geometry:

-Auto: let IceSL decide the best algorithm for
the given geometry.

-Discrete: robust method, performs well on highly
detailed shapes, can slice anything (STLs,
implicits, voxels), performance depends heavily on
GPU specs and object XY extent. Method of choice
for complex models.

-Polygonal: limited to meshes with no defects,
does not depend on GPU specs, performs well
regardless of object XY extent, demanding on
high polygon counts. Method of choice when slicing
a single STL.Sides of distance cone base
Number of sides of the regular polygon that makes up
the base of the 'bubbles' in the sponge structure.ÔªøNumber of contours in tower
Number of contours for the prime/wipe tower. Use more
if you notice that the extruder is not properly primed
(holes when the extruder resumes printing after a swap).
You may also change the tower size to push more plastic
during the priming of the extruder.ÔªøRaft
Prints a raft under the object to compensate for 
small calibration defects and to remove the printed 
part more easily afterwards.ÔªøSpeed change exponent on varying width
If single path fill is enabled, IceSL will consider 
printing tracks wider than the nozzle width. Depending 
on your printer and material, it may be necessary to 
slow down when printing larger than the nozzle. 
This exponent controls the slow down. For instance:
0.0 => no slow down
1.0 => linear, maintains constant extrusion speed
2.0 => four times slower if extruding twice the width
3.0 => eight times slower if extruding twice the width
We recommend 3.0 as a safe value.ÔªøSpeed for prime/retract (mm/sec)
Speed used for prime/retract extruder motions 
(millimeters per second). Decrease if the extruder
skips or grinds the filament after a retraction.ÔªøSave layer subset
Save only a subset of the produced layersÔªøRetraction speed after swap (mm/sec)
Speed of the filament retraction after an extruder swap.ÔªøExtrusion temperature (C)
Extruder temperature (Celsius) for this extruder.ÔªøTower size along Y (mm)
Size of the tower along Y direction. Increase if the 
extruder is not properly primed (holes when the 
extruder resumes printing after a swap), or if the 
tower is not stable enough when it becomes tall.ÔªøPrinter model
Select the printer model. It is possible to add 
new printers to IceSL, see subfolder gcode/ of
the install directory as well as the IceSL wiki.ÔªøLayer thickness (mm)
Layer thickness in millimeters (mm). Using smaller
values makes the part more precise and smooth, but
requires longer print times as the number of layers
increases. This parameter can be set per-layer 
to smoothly vary the thickness.ÔªøMax. unsupported bridge length (mm)
Maximum length in millimeters (mm) of an overhanging
bridge without support.ÔªøPrime/wipe tower
Instructs IceSL to build a prime/wipe tower structure 
alongside the print. The tower is used to wipe and prime
the extruders when switching material in multi-material
prints. The tower only prints when required (it will not
appear on single material prints).
The tower is either automatically placed, or its position
is controlled by variables 'extruder_swap_location_x_mm'
and 'extruder_swap_location_y_mm' when a swap 
location is specified in the printer scripts, and 
'extruder_swap_at_location' is enabled.
Whether to use a shield or a tower for multi-material 
prints depends on your printer: if the printer has nozzles 
that lift up/down, or a single nozzle with multiple 
filaments, use a tower. If it is a standard setup with
multiple fixed nozzles side by side, use a shield.ÔªøBrim
